<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luka Bych</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luka Bych was a Kuban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> politician who led the government of the Kuban People's Republic in 1918–1919. He belonged to the Ukrainian-minded faction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuban-rada">Kuban Rada</a> that favoured federation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and resisted General Denikin's aim of a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. He headed the Kuban delegation that sought recognition at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> in 1919 and later lived in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luka-bych.jpg" alt="Luka Bych" width="320" height="460" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luka_Lavrentievich_Bych.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
